WEBVTT

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:29.270
<v Ira Glass>I think we all have people in our lives who we love, but there's no talking to them. They have their way of seeing things or doing things, and it's hard to take. And no matter how you try to talk it out, it goes nowhere. It doesn't get solved, even if they also want things to change.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:51.980
<v Ira Glass>We're devoting our entire show today to a story like that from Zach Mack, who's a reporter. And the story is about him and his dad and how they both wanted to mend a rift that had grown between them that had lasted for years, but they couldn't figure out how until Zach's dad offered a very surprising way out.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:01:06.880
<v Ira Glass>That's going to be our whole show today-- his dad's unusual solution and how it played out. That's all I'm going to say for now. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And with that, I turn things over to Zach.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.740
<v Zach Mack>This all started about a year ago. My father called and left me this message.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:36.170
<v Dad>Hey, Zach. Dad again. Good conversation last week-- last night. Hey, I didn't remember to tell you this because I was kind of tired. But they're going to shut us down again. And it's going to be a worse shutdown than 2020 because of this EMP, that all the supply lines are going to be disrupted.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:56.710
<v Dad>So you really want to store up two months' worth of food and water because they probably won't be able to pump water throughout the city, because that's all controlled by computers and electronics. And the EMP is going to shut down everything electronic.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:07.500
<v Zach Mack>My dad's talking about something called an ElectroMagnetic Pulse, EMP for short, which I only know about because it's what George Clooney uses in the movie Ocean's Eleven to pull off the casino heist.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.810
<v Danny>What's a pinch?

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:18.710
<v Basher>A pinch is a device which creates like a cardiac arrest for any broadband electrical circuitry-- an electromagnetic pulse which shuts down any power source within its blast radius.

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:19.980
<v Dad>You should buy enough water.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:30.600
<v Zach Mack>I can't remember a single other time Dad left me a message expressing a safety concern, but he was really convinced this was going to happen. At one point, he even did a little math for how much water I would need to survive.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:48.230
<v Dad>So I would buy 60 times 10, which is 600 ounces. I know. I know it sounds like conspiracy theory, but they're going to do this. This is what they're planning. And I just want to have you be prepared. OK?

00:02:48.230 --> 00:03:01.630
<v Zach Mack>OK, so just a little background-- I wouldn't describe my father as a paranoid person. I wouldn't even call him an anxious person. He's actually an optimist and usually prefers to keep things light, so a lot of dad jokes.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:18.940
<v Zach Mack>What else? He's held down the same job forever. He has friends-- not close ones, but there's dudes he goes skiing and sailing with. When I was growing up, Dad was kind of in his own world. Mom was always the more involved parent, the one who knew all my friends and whatever was going on with me.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:35.920
<v Zach Mack>Dad and I have never been particularly close. Typically, when I hear from him on the phone, it's during an Ohio State football game. We both love the Buckeyes, so during a game, he'll call me, like, five or six times, but not much beyond games, though. Ohio State football is the one thing we can agree on.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:51.820
<v Zach Mack>The thing we can't seem to agree on is reality. Like so many people, Dad's gotten swept up in conspiracies. Lately, he's been talking about chemtrails, that the government can control the weather, that January 6 was staged by what he calls the "deep state."

00:03:51.820 --> 00:04:04.650
<v Zach Mack>As he's gotten more extreme in the last few years, I've seen my mom and sister retract and shut down around him. I'm typically the one who challenges Dad on this stuff. Because I'm a reporter, I can't resist taking the bait.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.230
<v Dad>It's called denying us freedom of speech.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:07.800
<v Zach Mack>No, no.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.020
<v Dad>It's called denying us freedom of speech.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.490
<v Zach Mack>It's misinformation.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:16.820
<v Dad>No. Who gets the right to label it misinformation?

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:41.810
<v Zach Mack>Of course, it never goes anywhere, until a year ago. After a family blowout over the holidays that was so bad, my sister and I left early, I decided to confront my father. I could see how his beliefs were starting to strain the family, and I worried that pretty soon, there may be no coming back for him. I told him I thought he was being radicalized online and pleaded with him to hear me.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:05:09.880
<v Zach Mack>As always, he didn't agree. But what he did next surprised me. Dad texted me a photo of a sheet of paper where, in his barely legible cursive, he had written out a list of 10 predictions, 10 things that he was positive would all take place sometime in 2024, assuring me that when all of these things happened, I would see once and for all that he was right. At the bottom of the page was a challenge to a bet for $10,000.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:29.410
<v Zach Mack>And let me just say, Dad and I are not big gamblers, nor are we rich. 10k is easily more money than either of us has ever wagered. Looking over the list, I was a combination of surprised, horrified, and also, it was a little hard to take seriously. I immediately called him up to discuss the terms of the bet.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.580
<v Zach Mack>Do you have the list in front of you? Because you--

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.280
<v Dad>Yeah, yeah.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:39.440
<v Zach Mack>Can you walk me through the 1 through 10? I just want to add-- each one, I'll probably have a couple clarifying questions.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:40.940
<v Dad>Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.540
<v Zach Mack>Keep in mind, this call took place back in January 2024.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:52.180
<v Dad>Number one, Obama will be found guilty of treason in 2024.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.270
<v Zach Mack>So, like, by a court?

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.200
<v Dad>Yes.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.010
<v Zach Mack>OK. Not the court of public opinion?

00:05:59.010 --> 00:05:59.910
<v Dad>Correct.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.770
<v Zach Mack>OK. And then number two?

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:09.660
<v Dad>Oh, yeah. Biden will be found guilty of treason in 2024.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.030
<v Zach Mack>Just treason for something specific?

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:17.790
<v Dad>For selling all those secrets that he had in the back of his Corvette.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:21.030
<v Zach Mack>I don't know if that's a joke. I'm not sure.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:25.870
<v Dad>It's partially a joke, but it's also real. I mean, it could be--

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:49.330
<v Zach Mack>It went on like this, all political. Dad's other predictions were that Nancy Pelosi would also be convicted of treason. Same for Bill and Hillary Clinton, who would go down for murder as well. That Trump would be reinstated without an election and cleared of all charges. That Biden would be removed from office. Same for both the governor and mayor of New York. And as a New York City resident, I didn't hate that last one.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:58.040
<v Zach Mack>That one, I'm actually kind of hopeful we get that one because I don't really love Eric Adams. He's kind of an idiot. So I'm with you on that one. [CHUCKLES]

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:02.790
<v Dad>Yep, he's a total idiot. And I'm impressed that you see that. So, good.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:03.790
<v Zach Mack>[CHUCKLES]

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:06.160
<v Dad>One we can both cheer for.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.940
<v Zach Mack>Eventually, we landed on his final prediction for 2024, his boldest one.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.740
<v Dad>Number 10-- I threw this in because I thought you'd be excited about it.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:18.520
<v Zach Mack>Yeah, it's an exciting one. It's probably the most exciting one.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:31.090
<v Dad>The US will come under martial law in 2024 because of mass rioting and chaos. So the military will have to step in.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:35.470
<v Zach Mack>For the record, I was never actually excited about the possibility of this happening.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:41.740
<v Zach Mack>Do you think this will be happening all over the country, only in certain parts?

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:54.370
<v Dad>Well, the whole United States will come under martial law. And the biggest cities will be targeted-- New York, unfortunately, Washington DC, the Bay Area.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:57.460
<v Zach Mack>OK. You seem pretty confident about that.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:59.850
<v Dad>I am 100% confident.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:04.410
<v Zach Mack>Because this is a pretty huge shake-up.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:05.220
<v Dad>Yeah.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:12.480
<v Zach Mack>I would say, our country hasn't seen anything like this since the Civil War, probably.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:25.290
<v Dad>Well, not even in the Civil War. This is going to be the biggest shake-up to ever hit the United States of America and the world at large.

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:31.470
<v Zach Mack>I mean, I think the way you've laid it out here, there'll be a pretty clear case of, like, who's right and who's wrong.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:35.790
<v Dad>Yeah. Yeah. I think it will be very black and white.

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:38.550
<v Zach Mack>And if you are wrong--

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:40.810
<v Dad>Then you're $10,000 richer.

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:51.370
<v Zach Mack>[CHUCKLES] Yeah, but look, this isn't just about the money, right? I would like to make some positive strides for our family.

00:08:51.370 --> 00:09:20.010
<v Dad>Well, I think that once you see that I haven't been duped by AI and social media and the algorithms, I think you will gain a lot more respect for who I am and how I think and how I've come to believe. The other thing I want to have you say after each one of these--

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:21.100
<v Zach Mack>Uh-huh.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:28.330
<v Dad>--too, is that, wow, Dad, you were right, and I was wrong.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.070
<v Zach Mack>Same goes for you then.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.730
<v Dad>OK. Absolutely.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.780
<v Zach Mack>All right. Deal. Happy to do it.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:42.820
<v Dad>All right.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:59.140
<v Zach Mack>And so our strange bet was on. I saw Dad's challenge as an opening. Instead of just arguing until the end of time, we were actually going to settle this. And now we had a deadline-- New Year's 2025. The moment I saw his proposal, I knew I had him beat.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:25.860
<v Zach Mack>And for the very first time, he'd have to admit he was wrong. All I had to do was wait 12 months, and once he decisively lost, I figured he'd be more open to my version of the truth, and maybe I could pull him back to reality a bit. And the timing for the bet couldn't be better because his beliefs were starting to tear our family apart.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:33.660
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, Zach calls his mom, who has some news of her own. Stay with us.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:39.690
<v Ira Glass>This American Life. Zach Mack picks up his story about his year-long bet with his dad.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:58.430
<v Zach Mack>Not long after making the bet, I called my mom. My parents have been married for 40 years. They met in their 20s in Chicago, fell in love, and moved to California, where they eventually had my sister and I. We grew up in the San Francisco Bay area, a pretty liberal place, which worked for my mom because she's a pretty liberal Jewish woman.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:19.770
<v Zach Mack>Dad has always been the odd man out, the lone Christian conservative, though growing up he was much quieter about his beliefs. And when my parents met, neither of them were religious. But Dad got back into it when I was young. Now he's very into it. That's been difficult for my parents for decades. But now, with the conspiracy stuff emerging, it's been really hard on my mom.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:41.570
<v Mom>It's getting pretty crazy, and I don't even know half of it. We have two generators in the house. There are now eight flats of water in the garage that he bought at Costco and some kind of fancy water filter system that was a couple hundred dollars. He's stockpiling weird food.

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:43.750
<v Zach Mack>What kind of food?

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:46.260
<v Mom>12 cans of canned chicken.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:47.360
<v Zach Mack>[CHUCKLES] OK.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:12:00.390
<v Mom>And I'm like, I have never eaten canned chicken. You have never eaten canned chicken. Why is there canned chicken? I feel like returning it all this week while he's not home. And he moved some money without my permission.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:04.200
<v Zach Mack>How did he do that? He didn't need your sign-off?

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:13.000
<v Mom>No, because it's a joint account. And he withdrew money from a joint account without telling me and made a purchase he knew I would never approve.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.640
<v Zach Mack>What was it?

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.290
<v Mom>He bought a precious metal.

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:23.190
<v Zach Mack>Because Dad's worried the banks are going to collapse, he wants to move big chunks of their money into things like platinum and silver.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:28.530
<v Zach Mack>Why is he so unwilling to listen to everyone around him?

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:42.570
<v Mom>I don't know. I wish I knew because then I could crack it. I don't know how to pull him back. I have been very clear that I'm not sure how much more I can take. And--

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:45.570
<v Zach Mack>Have you been clear with him about that?

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:52.880
<v Mom>Yeah, and I've even said, I'm considering leaving, ending the marriage.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:54.320
<v Zach Mack>You said that to him?

00:12:54.320 --> 00:13:04.980
<v Mom>Yeah. Which is very hard for me to say, because I'm 69. I'm about to retire.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:07.310
<v Zach Mack>Yeah.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:23.390
<v Mom>Not necessarily the life I had planned for myself, to be alone. But what kind of life do I have now?

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:33.810
<v Zach Mack>Mom was initially irritated by the bet because Dad didn't run it by her first, which happens a lot, but the idea of a deadline, that was starting to make sense to her.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:52.410
<v Mom>I need an endpoint. And so the endpoint is December 31. When January 1 comes around, things have to change. I'm only going to put up-- I can barely put up with it now, and I can't put up with it after that.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:14:16.860
<v Zach Mack>Later, I called my sister Kira about the bet. Unfortunately, she's been having her own conflict with Dad, also about his beliefs. Two years ago, Kira came out to him. My mom and I had known for years, but Kira was always afraid to tell Dad because she thought he'd disapprove. It didn't go well. He said he'd always love my sister, but he thought she was choosing to be gay, and he didn't agree with that choice.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:30.000
<v Kira>I think the hardest part is that he looked at me and told me that I was wrong about myself, and that he was right. And I don't understand how he could know that.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:42.510
<v Zach Mack>Kira and I are close. And when I told her about the bet, she liked the idea of forcing Dad to confront the reality that some of his beliefs are likely wrong. But we weren't sure if that helped her situation either way.

00:14:42.510 --> 00:15:07.580
<v Kira>So, like, let's say these things don't come true, and he's willing to say, like, I was wrong about these things, I don't feel like that would affect his beliefs around me. Like even if he's right about everything else, I'm probably still going to be gay after that.

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:30.080
<v Zach Mack>So Dad and I had a year. January came and went, and neither of us put any points on the board with the bet. Then in February, Dad caught a small break with Mayor Adams. He had been under investigation for months, and things were heating up. The FBI raided one of his advisors, and someone else pled guilty to conspiracy charges. It seemed like Dad could actually win that one.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:43.460
<v Zach Mack>Then in March, the New York governor sent the National Guard into the subway, which, if you squint, could look like the pretense to martial law. While we waited for the other predictions to come true or not, I tried to make the most of the time.

00:15:43.460 --> 00:16:03.640
<v Zach Mack>I found myself thinking back to something Dad said to me at the end of an argument once a few years back. He said, well, you don't really know me. And I wondered if he might be right, that maybe I didn't really know him. And if I had any hope of changing his mind or even nudging the trajectory of his beliefs, I would need to understand my father better.

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:28.120
<v Zach Mack>So I decided to make a project of it. One question I still had was how he'd even gotten here, though, looking back, there were signs. I remember my mom telling me about how when I was a child, Dad didn't want me to get vaccinated. Mom wasn't having it, so I ended up getting all my shots. But I never really understood where Dad's hangups came from. But now that we had some time to kill, I could ask him.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:35.240
<v Zach Mack>I know, throughout my lifetime, you have been distrusting of vaccines. Is that fair to say?

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.480
<v Dad>Yeah. Yeah, that's definitely true.

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:42.710
<v Zach Mack>I'm wondering when that began.

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:48.860
<v Dad>Well, the seed was planted, actually, before I went to school.

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:49.860
<v Zach Mack>As a child?

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:50.820
<v Dad>As a child.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:57.710
<v Zach Mack>Back in the '60s, Dad was about to enter elementary school in Ohio, and there was a new law requiring vaccines.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:12.030
<v Dad>Now, remember, Grandpa, my dad, was a chiropractor. And so he believed in natural immunity and the body's ability to heal itself. And so he was an anti-vaxxer.

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:13.270
<v Zach Mack>OK.

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:35.940
<v Dad>And I mean, we didn't call him that back then, but it's the same difference. And I just wanted to go to school, and I kept telling my dad and my mom, hey, it's OK, you know? I'm not afraid of the vaccination. I'm willing to go. And I just want to go to school. So I was not able to go to kindergarten because we couldn't find a school that would accept me without a vaccination.

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:38.880
<v Zach Mack>Oh, wow. So you didn't go to kindergarten?

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:41.000
<v Dad>I did not go to kindergarten.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:49.000
<v Zach Mack>Well, do you think your dad was-- I don't know. How would you describe him? Would you describe him as stubborn?

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:55.450
<v Dad>Oh, yeah. Stubborn, very opinionated, hard-headed at times.

00:17:55.450 --> 00:18:05.510
<v Zach Mack>Dad was one of six children. The family was supported primarily by my grandpa, who had a small chiropractic business. That is, until the Ohio State Medical Board cracked down on him.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:08.470
<v Dad>And so they literally threw my dad in jail for a couple of days--

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:09.010
<v Zach Mack>Wow.

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:11.440
<v Dad>--for practicing without a license.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:29.320
<v Zach Mack>The way Dad tells it, after my grandfather's business dissolved, he was never able to recover. He became so depressed that he literally couldn't get out of bed for, like, six months. And he was never the same man. That literally broke him.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.620
<v Zach Mack>Wow. And how old were you when this happened?

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:36.940
<v Dad>I was, like, 13 or 14 years old.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:56.570
<v Zach Mack>One of the things I remember most about my grandfather is how tall and thin he was, and how, one time when he came to visit us, his body had just completely changed. He had suddenly gained over 100 pounds in a very short amount of time. He was going through some mental health issues that the family never fully understood, and it was affecting his weight and his ability to sleep.

00:18:56.570 --> 00:19:10.890
<v Zach Mack>He wouldn't really sleep during the night, which meant that throughout the day, he would constantly fall asleep. He'd be in the middle of a conversation sometimes, or even at dinner, and he'd just start snoring. Minutes later, he'd abruptly wake up and carry on as if nothing happened.

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:28.430
<v Zach Mack>This also began to happen while he was driving. My grandpa got into a number of car accidents. He totaled several cars and nearly killed my grandmother. He refused to stop driving and even went to a neighboring state to get a driver's license after his was taken away. He refused to admit anything was wrong.

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:37.430
<v Zach Mack>The family tried to talk to him. So did my father several times. And when that didn't work-- and I'd never heard this part of the story before-- Dad tried something else.

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:52.570
<v Dad>I wrote him a long letter. I wrote him, like, a two or three-page letter and mailed it to him, because I wanted him to be able to read the whole letter so I could finish what I really wanted to say to him without interruption.

00:19:52.570 --> 00:20:19.120
<v Dad>And I said, Dad, I love you. I want the best for you. I'm not trying to say anything horrible or damaging or critical about you, but your whole family is concerned about you. Can't you see this? Can't you recognize that we all would like to see you live to a ripe old age? Because, I said, if you continue on this path, I don't know when you're going to die, but I know you're going to die. So.

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:33.910
<v Zach Mack>Not long after this-- I was 10 years old at the time-- my parents called my sister and me into the kitchen to tell us something. But before they said anything, I already knew. My grandfather was dead. His vehicle veered off the road and flipped over.

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:55.050
<v Zach Mack>When I think about my grandfather, I think about how what caused his death may have been stubbornness, a refusal to listen to those around him. He was 68 years old, the same age my father was when he proposed our bet.

00:20:55.050 --> 00:21:15.000
<v Zach Mack>As the year pressed on, we continued discussing Dad's life and the things he'd come to believe. Turns out, a lot of his predictions were coming from a single source online. And something you should know about my father is that he really struggles with technology. He can barely navigate the internet. He can't even figure out how to access his own email account from his laptop.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:38.710
<v Zach Mack>Around 2019, Dad got an iPad, and that felt like a turning point. It wasn't long until he started saying weird things. One time, I was home visiting, and we got into it because he was trying to tell me that the government was controlling Facebook from the very beginning, and that Mark Zuckerberg was just a pawn. And when the pandemic hit, it felt like the perfect storm. Like so many of us, Dad went searching for answers.

00:21:38.710 --> 00:22:02.500
<v Dad>I was googling around trying to find someone who I thought was reputable, reliable, and truly plugged into God. I was trying to find someone who was a legitimate prophet, because there's a lot of false prophets out there.

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:16.450
<v Zach Mack>What Dad found was a lot of conspiracies, of course, but also the next evolution of his faith. During the pandemic, Dad came to believe strongly in prophecy, the idea that God still speaks through intermediaries.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:28.160
<v Zach Mack>When you're talking about how God speaks today and you believe it's through prophets, that is a divisive within Christianity, right? That is a divisive thought?

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:30.130
<v Dad>Very divisive.

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:35.700
<v Zach Mack>Why do you believe it?

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:58.980
<v Dad>Because he's always had prophets. And it makes sense that just like he had profits in Old Testament times, he would continue with it. Why would he suddenly stop? I never quite understood why God would stop talking to his people.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:03.720
<v Zach Mack>Sure enough, Dad found someone he believes can commune directly with God.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:07.210
<v Julie Green>I am the great revealer.

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:10.510
<v Zach Mack>Meet Julie Green, my dad's preferred prophet.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:19.870
<v Julie Green>A great fall is coming, a fall of a global cabal. My children, 2024 will be a year like no other.

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:23.970
<v Dad>I firmly believe that God did lead me to Julie Green.

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:38.300
<v Zach Mack>She's not particularly big online, a couple 100,000 subscribers, but she's part of a growing movement within Christianity that emphasizes spiritual warfare and politics. It's all very Trumpy and full of prophecies.

00:23:38.300 --> 00:23:47.200
<v Julie Green>Secrets in the White House are about to be revealed. The Obamas, the Clintons, Adam Schiff, Mitch McConnell, Chuck Schumer, Nancy Pelosi--

00:23:47.200 --> 00:24:09.100
<v Zach Mack>As you can probably tell, this is where Dad's getting most of his predictions. And when I talk to Dad about these predictions, it's clear that for him, they all stem from his religious beliefs, which I've always found somewhat confusing because when I look at his list, none of these things seem to have anything to do with God at all.

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:23.350
<v Zach Mack>In July, some news-- President Biden announced he was withdrawing as the Democratic nominee for president. I thought, what was Dad's prediction again? Oh, he said, removed from office. Guess I'm in the clear.

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:43.510
<v Zach Mack>As the year sped by, I read books with titles like When Prophecy Fails. I spoke to a number of clinicians and conspiracy experts, met my dad's pastors. I even interviewed several of his friends. None of them shared his beliefs, but they said they didn't really argue with him about it either. Only one said it hurt their friendship with Dad.

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:53.150
<v Zach Mack>Sometimes I'd send Dad highlights of articles debunking some of his more out-there claims, like the one about how the Joe Biden you see on the news is actually a body double.

00:24:53.150 --> 00:25:15.050
<v Dad>Well, it's actually a common practice in a lot of third world countries. Look at Saddam Hussein. Saddam Hussein had-- they aren't even sure, but over a half a dozen body doubles. That's why he could appear in one part of the country one day and someplace else another day.

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:20.750
<v Zach Mack>Yeah, I don't think they were giving speeches and making policy on his behalf, though. You know what I'm saying?

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:20.800
<v Dad>Some of them were.

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:22.150
<v Zach Mack>I think they were just maybe like--

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:22.630
<v Dad>Some of them were.

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:23.390
<v Zach Mack>--a little quick appearance.

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:32.740
<v Dad>But mostly, yeah, they were just appearances. Fun fact, while we're talking about body doubles, when the Biden--

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:34.830
<v Zach Mack>Why do you call him "the Biden"?

00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:36.820
<v Dad>Well, because it's a body double.

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:38.210
<v Zach Mack>[CHUCKLES] OK.

00:25:38.210 --> 00:26:17.340
<v Dad>The last time Netanyahu, who's the Israeli prime minister, came to visit the US here in Washington, DC, it was interesting that the fake Biden stood 6 inches taller than Netanyahu. Netanyahu and Biden are both listed to be 5 feet, 10 inches tall. So don't you find it interesting that when they were standing side by side in a couple of photos, that Biden was suddenly 6 inches taller?

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:26.100
<v Zach Mack>Well, first of all, Biden's listed height is 6 feet. Let's see what Netanyahu's height is. He's also 6 feet, it says.

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:32.400
<v Dad>OK, yeah, they're the same height. The point is, they're the same height. I thought it was 5' 10". So yeah. So it's 6 feet.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:34.620
<v Zach Mack>Are you talking about this image?

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:36.480
<v Dad>Yeah, I think that's it. Yeah.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:42.100
<v Zach Mack>He looks a little taller. Well, but he's taller in every picture. This is a different place.

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:43.330
<v Dad>Are they the same height?

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:44.730
<v Zach Mack>They don't look the same height in this picture.

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:48.030
<v Dad>OK, that's my point. They are the same height.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:56.150
<v Zach Mack>Well, people lie about their heights all the time, right? So I'm sure-- Allen Iverson was always listed as 6' 1". He was actually 5' 10", right? People--

00:26:56.150 --> 00:27:00.610
<v Dad>I get that. I get that. But presidents don't do this.

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:25.170
<v Zach Mack>My fact-checker wants me to point out here that we don't actually know Allen Iverson's real height, or Netanyahu's height, or whether or not Saddam Hussein had body doubles. But this is the joy of debating a conspiracy theorist. Inevitably, you run out of patience or just don't know the facts well enough to continue pushing back. So whenever I debate Dad, it feels like I can never do enough research or compile enough evidence.

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:52.470
<v Zach Mack>Later in the summer, I was reading a science journal and saw a possible way around that. This research group was doing some experiments where they had some conspiracy theorists engage in conversations with AI chatbots, which had all the facts, could push back gently, and unlike me, had infinite patience. The data on its effectiveness looked pretty promising, so I reached out and actually got them to program something special just for my dad to try, which he did for several minutes.

00:27:52.470 --> 00:27:59.480
<v Dad>To be honest, it didn't say anything that I hadn't already heard.

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:09.020
<v Zach Mack>Swing and a miss. But the work continues.

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:41.160
<v Zach Mack>In August, Dad got it in his head that Kamala Harris was going to be replaced as the Democratic nominee at the convention. And out of nowhere, he made a second political conspiracy bet, this time with my mom. The terms-- if Mom lost, she'd have to give up, as he put it, all mainstream media for the rest of the year. And if he lost, he'd have to stop watching Julie Green entirely, his favorite prophet. Much to Mom and I's delight, he lost that bet a few days later when Harris officially accepted the nomination.

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:45.240
<v Mom>Your father's in his silk robe, and he's got a cocktail.

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:46.720
<v Zach Mack>Yeah, drink up, buddy.

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:49.140
<v Zach Mack>[LAUGHTER]

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:54.240
<v Zach Mack>So Dad, so no more Julie Green till the end of the year?

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:56.340
<v Dad>Correct.

00:28:56.340 --> 00:29:00.330
<v Zach Mack>How do you feel about that?

00:29:00.330 --> 00:29:05.280
<v Dad>I'm disappointed, but it's OK. I have other resources.

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:14.460
<v Zach Mack>It did feel like a relief that he wouldn't be watching Julie Green anymore. And him admitting he was wrong? That was something.

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:19.500
<v Zach Mack>By the fall, Dad and I were speaking more than ever and in ways we never have.

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:22.930
<v Zach Mack>Do you feel like I know you now better?

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:33.590
<v Dad>Oh, absolutely. Yeah, you know me, these conversations and just talking about my dad and all the rest, absolutely.

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:38.260
<v Ira Glass>And of course, football season was kicking off, so there were lots of calls and voicemails about Ohio State.

00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:38.960
<v Ira Glass>[CHEERING]

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:43.730
<v Announcer>Three penalties on this drive against Ohio State, and now Johnson waiting back.

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:49.970
<v Dad>They should have thrown on first down or at least second down. Anyway, I'll talk to you at halftime.

00:29:49.970 --> 00:29:55.040
<v Ira Glass>Dad and I were getting along better than ever, but time on our bet was running out.

00:29:55.040 --> 00:30:00.680
<v Dad>We're down to the last four months, last third of the year.

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:03.770
<v Zach Mack>How do you feel about that?

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:17.350
<v Dad>Um, impatient. I really thought more would have happened by now.

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:25.940
<v Zach Mack>I don't think I ever asked you this, and I should have asked you-- why did you challenge me to this bet?

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:33.650
<v Dad>Oh, why did I challenge you to this bet? That's a good question. I wanted to get your attention.

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:34.970
<v Zach Mack>Like, what do you mean by that?

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:57.370
<v Dad>Well, because I wanted you to believe that God is still active and alive and still in control of the world. And I thought this would be a concrete way for you to see that and experience that.

00:30:57.370 --> 00:31:08.410
<v Zach Mack>Man, I didn't think about it that way. So it's sort of about you wanting me to get closer to God.

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:11.800
<v Dad>Yeah.

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:20.720
<v Zach Mack>I mean, I think that's very touching. I think you're super wrong about all these predictions, but that your reasoning is very touching.

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:22.150
<v Dad>OK.

00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:29.670
<v Zach Mack>I appreciate that. Anyways, OK. Let's end on a high note.

00:31:29.670 --> 00:31:32.370
<v Dad>OK, sounds good. We'll talk to you soon.

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:34.190
<v Zach Mack>Sounds good. Love you guys.

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:35.580
<v Dad>Hey, and you know what, Zach?

00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:36.200
<v Zach Mack>Huh?

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:39.770
<v Dad>This alone has been well worth it. All the conversations--

00:31:39.770 --> 00:31:40.160
<v Zach Mack>Oh.

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:51.830
<v Dad>--we've had, this alone, I mean, to be perfectly honest, if I had to pay you 50 grand for the time we've spent together, every penny has been worth it.

00:31:51.830 --> 00:32:05.870
<v Zach Mack>Dad, well, I can't handle you saying things like that. This is too nice. This is too sweet. It's too sweet. I appreciate it, Dad. It's actually been really nice talking to you, too.

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:06.860
<v Dad>All right.

00:32:06.860 --> 00:32:07.560
<v Zach Mack>All right.

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:08.160
<v Dad>Take care.

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:19.730
<v Zach Mack>You, too. Good night.

00:32:19.730 --> 00:32:35.840
<v Ira Glass>As we stretch into November, Dad, Mom and I got hit with some sad news. After a year of nearly no contact with my father, my sister informed the family that she wouldn't be coming home for the holidays, which, in my lifetime, I count only three times we've ever missed the holidays together.

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:38.630
<v Zach Mack>Why aren't you coming home for Christmas?

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:48.510
<v Kira>Because I don't want to be there. Oh. I feel like I'm going to cry. [SIGHS]

00:32:48.510 --> 00:33:20.760
<v Kira>(EMOTIONALLY) I think last year was so terrible, incredibly terrible for me. And the thought of being back in that space feels awful. Why would I choose that? I'm not going to get OK with him believing that I'm somebody that I'm not and wanting so badly for me to be something else.

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:39.000
<v Kira>And it's hard because I say that, and then I immediately think, well, that's what I want from him. I want him to fundamentally change who he is and be a different person. That's where I feel stuck is, like, we want the same things from each other. And I don't know if that's possible.

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:54.190
<v Zach Mack>I don't know either. The bet was nearly over, but it felt like our family was barely hanging on. Then December came. It was just days before Christmas, days before I was flying home and Mom called me with another update.

00:33:54.190 --> 00:33:56.730
<v Zach Mack>Hey, how are you?

00:33:56.730 --> 00:34:02.190
<v Mom>A lot going on in the last 12 hours. [SIGHS]

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:07.810
<v Zach Mack>She began to fill me in. The night before, Mom and Dad were talking about the state of the family over dinner.

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:28.280
<v Mom>And I told him that he was welcome to come Christmas Eve, but that, likely, Christmas Day, you and I would do something by ourselves. And he got very upset.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:35:08.650
<v Mom>And we had a-- I mean, I can't remember exactly all the parts of the conversation, but I said, the reasoning, how I got to this place was the situation with Kira. And then I was tolerant of his religious journey. I can be tolerant of political differences. But when he started following the YouTube shit--

00:35:08.650 --> 00:35:09.500
<v Zach Mack>Prophet.

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:36.460
<v Mom>The prophet stuff and what I feel is fake news and all of the doom and gloom and generators and flats of water and granola bars all over the place, I said, I just-- I can't anymore. I just can't anymore. Our 40-year history is just not enough for me. We are already living very, very separate lives.

00:35:36.460 --> 00:35:37.690
<v Zach Mack>Separate realities.

00:35:37.690 --> 00:35:56.730
<v Mom>Yeah, and separate realities. I didn't say that. He said that he's not going to change his religious viewpoints. He's not going to change. And then I just said it. And I just don't see a path where this marriage can continue. I don't see a path for me.

00:35:56.730 --> 00:36:00.690
<v Zach Mack>And what did he say?

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:04.500
<v Mom>He sort of didn't really respond.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:14.370
<v Zach Mack>After 40 years of marriage, they had agreed to separate and for my dad to move out. Dad's beliefs around Kira, the conspiracies, it had just become too much.

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:39.790
<v Mom>I'm embarrassed to say the situation with Kira wasn't the one that knocked it over. But I was ever hopeful that he would come to a reality about that. But what I realized is that he's beyond redemption at this point.

00:36:39.790 --> 00:37:18.490
<v Mom>There's this little piece of me that is like, you have this conversation with him on January 1, his list is debunked. He will realize, if he steps back and looks at the whole picture, that he's not grounded in any reality, and that he'll have an awakening, and I will have a marriage and a family.

00:37:18.490 --> 00:37:54.270
<v Zach Mack>So this was the situation I flew home to. Things felt weird. I spent the week crashing at friends' houses and in hotels. Mom slept in Kira's room. Christmas Day, as you can imagine, was sad. There were no gifts exchanged. Mom and I just went to the movies and ate Chinese food. I'm not even sure what my father or sister got up to that day. A few days later, Dad said he was ready to sit down and close out the bet.

00:37:54.270 --> 00:38:12.420
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, somebody's going to need to say, as promised, you were right, I was wrong, which can be such a powerful thing in any relationship. That's in a minute, Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.

00:38:12.420 --> 00:38:28.220
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Our show today, 10 things I don't want to hate about you, about Zach Mack and his year-long bet with his dad. We've arrived at the end of December. Zach's family is not doing well. And there's still the bet.

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:43.820
<v Zach Mack>I know this sounds crazy because as bad as things had gotten, Dad was still excited about settling up. I had no idea what he was going to say. But as we often are, we started off pretty playfully, which is our way of postponing the hard stuff.

00:38:43.820 --> 00:38:46.200
<v Zach Mack>All right. OK, here we go.

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:51.900
<v Dad>All right. Hand me the wine here. Oh, I should go get my list, right?

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:53.570
<v Zach Mack>Yeah, go get your list.

00:38:53.570 --> 00:39:02.300
<v Dad>Yeah, let me just have it, too, so I can look at it and see if there's anything I can pretend happened.

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:12.420
<v Zach Mack>Well, at least so far, we're sharing a reality. OK, I'm just going to go through the list for your 10 predictions for the year. We'll start there.

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:12.770
<v Dad>OK.

00:39:12.770 --> 00:39:29.300
<v Zach Mack>We'll start with the easy ones. There's four that can easily be grouped together. And that is Barack Obama will be convicted of treason, Joe Biden will be convicted of treason, Nancy Pelosi convicted of treason, the Clintons convicted of treason and murder. Right? Those are all kind of-- I would group them categorically.

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:30.090
<v Dad>Right, yeah.

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:37.120
<v Zach Mack>I began-- no investigations, no charges, no convictions, literally no momentum of any kind.

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:46.000
<v Dad>And yes, I fully hoped that would take place before the end of 2024, but that has not taken place.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:48.670
<v Zach Mack>If you're keeping score at home, that's four for me.

00:39:48.670 --> 00:40:10.990
<v Zach Mack>All right, next one. You said Trump will be reinstated without an election. Trump won reelection. He was not reinstated without an election. That's five. Next prediction, Trump will have all charges dropped. On May 30, 2024, Trump was found guilty and convicted on 34 felony charges in the New York hush money trial.

00:40:10.990 --> 00:40:11.700
<v Dad>Yeah.

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:19.380
<v Zach Mack>To be fair to Dad, charges in two other cases were dropped, but Dad's prediction was all charges, so that makes six for me.

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:34.760
<v Zach Mack>Moving on, next one, you said Governor Hochul, the governor of New York, would be removed from office. Governor Hochul is currently in office with no charges being brought against her. She will be up for reelection in 2026.

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:36.330
<v Dad>That is correct.

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:41.190
<v Zach Mack>Seven. Onto the home stretch-- Biden will be removed from office.

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:44.970
<v Zach Mack>So Biden's still in the White House. You're not rebutting that.

00:40:44.970 --> 00:40:49.450
<v Dad>Yeah, well, because somebody pretending to be the Biden is in the White House.

00:40:49.450 --> 00:40:49.710
<v Zach Mack>OK.

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:50.200
<v Zach Mack>[CHUCKLING]

00:40:50.200 --> 00:41:06.740
<v Ira Glass>And we're back to body doubles. But even still, he conceded the point, so that's eight. OK, then there's Eric Adams, the mayor of New York, who Dad predicted would be removed from office, which still might happen. But Dad said by the end of 2024.

00:41:06.740 --> 00:41:09.840
<v Zach Mack>As it stands right now, he's still the mayor.

00:41:09.840 --> 00:41:10.970
<v Dad>Right.

00:41:10.970 --> 00:41:14.550
<v Zach Mack>Tough break, but that makes nine. OK, last one--

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:19.070
<v Zach Mack>Our country would come under martial law. As far as I know, that didn't happen.

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:20.210
<v Dad>That did not happen.

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:22.160
<v Zach Mack>OK. Any rebuttals there?

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:25.250
<v Dad>I'm very happy that didn't happen.

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:27.230
<v Zach Mack>And that makes 10.

00:41:27.230 --> 00:41:28.670
<v Dad>However--

00:41:28.670 --> 00:41:29.600
<v Zach Mack>Oh, no.

00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:39.710
<v Dad>--I still see that as a strong possibility of happening. I think it still has a strong possibility of happening within the next two months.

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:41.080
<v Zach Mack>Here we go.

00:41:41.080 --> 00:41:45.410
<v Dad>And I still am 100% positive that all these are true.

00:41:45.410 --> 00:41:47.590
<v Zach Mack>Even though none of them came true?

00:41:47.590 --> 00:41:53.470
<v Dad>Even though none of them have been actualized by the end of 2024.

00:41:53.470 --> 00:42:01.330
<v Zach Mack>So your explanation for why these things did not yet come to pass is that you just got the timeline wrong?

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:02.480
<v Dad>Yes.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:10.070
<v Zach Mack>We're sort of starting where we're ending, which is you still believe what you believe, and I still believe that that's false.

00:42:10.070 --> 00:42:15.830
<v Dad>Correct.

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:22.290
<v Zach Mack>I had waited for so long to hear him say the magic words we agreed to a year ago. Now the moment finally came.

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:27.690
<v Dad>Wow, Zach, I was wrong. And, um-- and what?

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:29.930
<v Zach Mack>And not only were you wrong--

00:42:29.930 --> 00:42:32.840
<v Dad>Oh, and you were correct.

00:42:32.840 --> 00:42:42.860
<v Zach Mack>To tell you the truth, hearing this was completely unsatisfying because I knew he didn't really believe it. I had won the bet, but it didn't really feel like winning.

00:42:42.860 --> 00:42:50.810
<v Zach Mack>I just see an unwillingness for you to be wrong.

00:42:50.810 --> 00:42:57.810
<v Dad>Wrong about 10 things. I'm going to admit, I was wrong about the timeline on all 10 things. I've been wrong--

00:42:57.810 --> 00:42:58.740
<v Zach Mack>But you see how you prefaced it?

00:42:58.740 --> 00:42:59.270
<v Dad>I've been--

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:02.850
<v Zach Mack>Not that you're wrong about them. You're just wrong about the timeline.

00:43:02.850 --> 00:43:05.130
<v Dad>Yeah. But that's still a type of wrong.

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:08.360
<v Zach Mack>Sure, but it's a half measure. It's a way for you to be right.

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:24.370
<v Ira Glass>I can't say I was surprised. All year long, I had read about how unlikely it is to pull someone away from these kinds of beliefs, how when believers of a prophecy witness it not come to pass, that oftentimes, they inexplicably double down, which was exactly what he was doing.

00:43:24.370 --> 00:43:31.360
<v Zach Mack>All of these beliefs, it feels like you're really holding on to them. And I'm not sure why.

00:43:31.360 --> 00:43:58.930
<v Dad>Because I know they're true. Why am I going to abandon truth? Why would I believe a lie? Why would I give that up? And it's not an arrogant thing. It's a knowing in my soul and in my spirit. So yeah, I mean, I can't abandon the truth.

00:43:58.930 --> 00:44:07.570
<v Zach Mack>I was getting nowhere, and it was frustrating. But I thought, this is history repeating itself. Surely, he could see that, right?

00:44:07.570 --> 00:44:36.900
<v Zach Mack>One of the things I've thought a lot about during this process is your dad and what happened to him. And in the face of so many people around him, including yourself, saying, "Hey, you're going down the wrong road. Don't do this. Please listen," and he wouldn't. He wouldn't hear it from anyone. And it cost him his life. I see a lot of parallels with what's happening right now.

00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:38.450
<v Dad>Interesting.

00:44:38.450 --> 00:44:43.890
<v Zach Mack>I don't think you're at risk of physical danger so much.

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:44.210
<v Dad>Yeah.

00:44:44.210 --> 00:44:51.800
<v Zach Mack>I think you're at risk of a lot of other things.

00:44:51.800 --> 00:44:53.850
<v Dad>Like what?

00:44:53.850 --> 00:45:06.260
<v Zach Mack>[SIGHS] Financial ruin. I think you're at risk of being ostracized and completely left out of this family dynamic.

00:45:06.260 --> 00:45:09.740
<v Dad>That would break my heart. But I see that.

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:13.420
<v Zach Mack>I think you're at risk reputationally.

00:45:13.420 --> 00:45:15.340
<v Dad>How so?

00:45:15.340 --> 00:45:27.130
<v Zach Mack>I feel like the closest friends in your life, your family, the people around you are telling you, hey, you're going the wrong way, and you're not listening.

00:45:27.130 --> 00:45:30.070
<v Dad>Actually, none of them are saying that to me.

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:30.890
<v Zach Mack>None of them?

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:32.800
<v Dad>None of them.

00:45:32.800 --> 00:45:34.170
<v Zach Mack>OK.

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:39.700
<v Dad>The only people who've said that are you and Mom.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:44.320
<v Zach Mack>So if all those people told you, would you hear them?

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:53.530
<v Dad>I would listen to them. I probably wouldn't change, but I'd listen to them. I'd take it into consideration.

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:54.400
<v Zach Mack>[SIGHS]

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:55.750
<v Dad>So.

00:45:55.750 --> 00:46:00.080
<v Zach Mack>You don't think that sounds like your dad?

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:08.870
<v Dad>That aspect of it, yeah. But my dad, my dad had trouble living in reality. I don't have trouble living in reality.

00:46:08.870 --> 00:46:21.230
<v Zach Mack>I would argue that you do. I mean, look at the state of our family right now. And I think we've gotten to this place in large part because of your beliefs.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:45.630
<v Dad>Well, we've gotten here completely because of a difference in beliefs. Yeah, you can point the finger at me and say, it's because of your beliefs. Well, that's partially true, but it's simultaneously because of the three of your beliefs, right?

00:46:45.630 --> 00:47:08.610
<v Dad>And I understand your beliefs, but I don't believe that way. I'm not going to pretend I believe that way. And I know you guys don't want me just to pretend. You want me to embrace it and live it and think it and breathe it, the same way you do. I can't do that.

00:47:08.610 --> 00:47:09.340
<v Zach Mack>[SIGHS]

00:47:09.340 --> 00:47:12.770
<v Dad>Therefore, it creates a wedge, right?

00:47:12.770 --> 00:47:13.600
<v Zach Mack>Yeah.

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:25.160
<v Dad>But it doesn't have to. I think the post-COVID years have really magnified and polarized people.

00:47:25.160 --> 00:47:44.050
<v Zach Mack>Yeah. But I would say that's when you got radicalized. I don't think, ultimately, Mom, Kira, and I have changed a lot ideologically since that time. I don't think you're in the same place.

00:47:44.050 --> 00:47:55.770
<v Dad>I can agree with that. I see that. I have made more significant changes in the past four years in terms of my own spiritual awareness and spiritual growth.

00:47:55.770 --> 00:47:56.980
<v Zach Mack>[SIGHS] Yeah.

00:47:56.980 --> 00:48:06.850
<v Dad>So and that certainly, I know, feels and looks very radical.

00:48:06.850 --> 00:48:10.080
<v Zach Mack>Let's talk about Mom for a second.

00:48:10.080 --> 00:48:22.740
<v Dad>Oh, I think your mom-- I don't know what I think about her anymore. [CHUCKLES] I mean, I don't think negative about her, other than she's lost and confused.

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:25.600
<v Zach Mack>I mean, I guess she would say that about you.

00:48:25.600 --> 00:48:56.680
<v Dad>Probably, and a lot worse. I continue to love your mom, but this is the irony. She sits in judgment of me and therefore judges me unworthy of being her partner and husband anymore. I mean, short of pretending to be somebody who I'm not, I don't see a solution.

00:48:56.680 --> 00:49:02.370
<v Zach Mack>Yeah. I agree.

00:49:02.370 --> 00:49:09.770
<v Ira Glass>I watched his frustration rise as he came up empty over and over, slowly realizing there was no fixing this.

00:49:09.770 --> 00:49:36.550
<v Zach Mack>I mean, look, I think this year has been difficult, especially recently. I do feel like we are getting along really well. I do feel conflicted because as that's happening, you and Mom are getting separated, and Kira is not home for Christmas. And those are all because of your beliefs.

00:49:36.550 --> 00:49:48.610
<v Dad>I can accept you and love you and have my beliefs and accept your beliefs. You can't accept my beliefs without judging me.

00:49:48.610 --> 00:49:50.270
<v Zach Mack>You don't think you're judging Kira?

00:49:50.270 --> 00:49:53.990
<v Dad>Not at all. I love my daughter. I love Kira.

00:49:53.990 --> 00:49:54.890
<v Zach Mack>I know that you do.

00:49:54.890 --> 00:49:58.990
<v Dad>I would never, I would never disown my daughter.

00:49:58.990 --> 00:50:03.710
<v Zach Mack>But I think Kira does feel rejected. She does not feel fully accepted by you.

00:50:03.710 --> 00:50:21.420
<v Dad>Yeah, I get that. It's not about rejection. See, that's how you guys label it. But it's not rejection. It's about looking at it from a spiritual viewpoint where this is not what God wants for her.

00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:32.400
<v Zach Mack>As the conversation wound down, I let go of any notion that I could convince him of anything. I was finally ready to stop struggling and just accept the truth. I couldn't help him.

00:50:32.400 --> 00:51:12.970
<v Zach Mack>I just want to say, I do really appreciate-- ooh, man, sorry. [SNIFFLES, SIGHS] It's been-- it's been a hard year, man. Yeah. I mean, I appreciate the conversations we've had all year. Like, I do feel-- I don't know. I feel closer to you than I ever have. And I'm really sad about the state of this family.

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:13.460
<v Dad>Yeah.

00:51:13.460 --> 00:51:27.400
<v Zach Mack>But I do appreciate your openness, your willingness to-- [CRYING] you never ducked. You never ducked a hard question.

00:51:27.400 --> 00:51:30.100
<v Dad>No need to.

00:51:30.100 --> 00:51:35.440
<v Zach Mack>[SNIFFLES, SIGHS]

00:51:35.440 --> 00:51:42.130
<v Zach Mack>I don't know. I mean-- [SNIFFLES] where do you think we'll be next Christmas?

00:51:42.130 --> 00:51:54.830
<v Dad>I-- [LAUGHS] I don't know. I have no idea. I have no idea, man. [SIGHS] I can't even think that far ahead.

00:51:54.830 --> 00:51:59.900
<v Zach Mack>Yeah. Yeah, I have one final question for you.

00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:01.570
<v Dad>OK. [CHUCKLES]

00:52:01.570 --> 00:52:04.820
<v Zach Mack>How are you going to pay me? [CHUCKLES]

00:52:04.820 --> 00:52:05.670
<v Dad>Um--

00:52:05.670 --> 00:52:12.720
<v Zach Mack>Check? Should we go to the bank? Should I get one of those giant golf checks? [LAUGHS]

00:52:12.720 --> 00:52:15.910
<v Dad>I figured you'd probably want small, unmarked bills.

00:52:15.910 --> 00:52:16.080
<v Zach Mack>Yeah.

00:52:16.080 --> 00:52:17.050
<v Dad>Nonsequential.

00:52:17.050 --> 00:52:17.340
<v Zach Mack>Yes.

00:52:17.340 --> 00:52:19.780
<v Dad>So did you bring a suitcase?

00:52:19.780 --> 00:52:21.880
<v Zach Mack>Yeah, I have a gym bag.

00:52:21.880 --> 00:52:24.160
<v Dad>OK. We'll fill that puppy up.

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:25.260
<v Zach Mack>Let's do it.

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:30.770
<v Ira Glass>We made the bet a year ago, but I've waited a lot longer than that to have this conversation with him.

00:52:30.770 --> 00:52:34.010
<v Zach Mack>[SIGHS] All right. Let's get a hug.

00:52:34.010 --> 00:52:39.580
<v Dad>(CHUCKLING) All right. Oh, I love you.

00:52:39.580 --> 00:52:41.820
<v Zach Mack>I love you, too.

00:52:41.820 --> 00:52:44.950
<v Ira Glass>As we stood there hugging, I just broke down.

00:52:44.950 --> 00:53:03.120
<v Zach Mack>I'm sorry. I'm sorry it's been-- [CRYING]

00:53:03.120 --> 00:53:26.900
<v Ira Glass>I don't know what comes next, where the family goes from here. It felt like things fell apart so quickly, but also slowly over many years. I think in many ways, I'm the one who got off the easiest. I'm still in good standing with everyone, which makes me feel a mixture of relief and guilt. And if you're wondering, yes, I took the money. Absolutely.

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:29.550
<v Zach Mack>OK. Now we're walking to the Rose Bowl.

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:30.020
<v Dad>Yeah.

00:53:30.020 --> 00:53:31.050
<v Zach Mack>How do you feel?

00:53:31.050 --> 00:53:34.590
<v Ira Glass>But on New Year's Day, the day my dad officially lost the bet--

00:53:34.590 --> 00:53:35.090
<v Dad>I'm pumped up.

00:53:35.090 --> 00:53:39.120
<v Zach Mack>--I took him to go see Ohio State, our favorite football team, play in the Rose Bowl--

00:53:39.120 --> 00:53:40.590
<v Zach Mack>What do you think the score is going to be?

00:53:40.590 --> 00:53:42.210
<v Ira Glass>--and paid for everything.

00:53:42.210 --> 00:53:49.140
<v Dad>I think it's going to be 31 to 22, Bucks.

00:53:49.140 --> 00:53:55.430
<v Zach Mack>Yet another prediction my father got wrong, by the way. But they did win. Go, Buckeyes.

00:53:55.430 --> 00:54:01.670
<v Dad>Let's go! All right! Let's go!

00:54:01.670 --> 00:54:31.860
<v Zach Mack>It felt good to treat dad with his own money and to agree on the one reality we've always shared-- football-- maybe the only one we ever will. As I watched the clock run down, I knew that the moment the game was over, we'd have to face the fact that, as a family, in the end, we didn't make it.

00:54:31.860 --> 00:54:49.770
<v Ira Glass>Zach Mack. He's a producer at Vox Media. His story was a collaboration with NPR's Embedded podcast, which, if you haven't heard them, you should check them out. They have lots of documentary stories like this. David Kestenbaum, on our staff, worked on the story here. The staff who worked on it there were producers Dan Girma and Ariana Gharib Lee.

00:54:49.770 --> 00:55:01.030
<v Ira Glass>Editing by Luis Trelles. Katie Simon is Embedded's showrunner. Some original music in this episode was composed by Peter Leonard. Fact-checking by Gretta Pittenger on the Embedded staff and Christopher Swetala on our staff.

00:55:01.030 --> 00:55:30.950
<v Ira Glass>NPR's Embedded is releasing a three-part series about Zach's bet with his dad that gets into material that we did not have time for here, including some revealing conversations with his dad's friends and some more interesting stuff from Zach and his dad. It is available now. You can find Embedded wherever you get your podcasts.

00:55:30.950 --> 00:55:51.480
<v Ira Glass>Well, today's episode was produced by Lilly Sullivan. The people who put together today's show include Phia Bennin, Michael Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Angela Gervasi, Cassie Howley, Chana Joffe-Walt, Seth Lind, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Frances Swanson, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry. Special thanks today to Avery Trufelman.

